<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="grv-ch-fields">
  <title>Fields and characteristic</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>Many characteristic-zero constructions can be written over a finitely generated field and studied over <m>\C</m>. This does not by itself identify Grothendieck rings over different fields: extending scalars changes the available classes as well as their invariants. We begin with the finite-data argument that makes base change injective over an algebraically closed field. The Abel–Jacobi map of a curve then shows how a projective-bundle calculation changes when there is no rational point. We also examine complex models and the geometric inputs that depend on characteristic.</p>
  </introduction>
  <section xml:id="grf-basechange">
    <title>Base change and finite scissors identities</title>
    <p xml:id="grf-ring-conventions">Let <m>k</m> be any field, and write
      <md number="no">R_k=K_0(\Var_k),\qquad \LL_k=[\mathbb A^1_k],\qquad \mathcal M_k=R_k[\LL_k^{-1}].</md>
      Varieties are reduced separated finite-type schemes. When a product or base change is not reduced, take its reduction. Equivalently, one can allow all separated finite-type schemes and all scissors relations: the closed immersion of the reduction has empty complement and gives <m>[X]=[X_{\mathrm{red}}]</m>. This also permits specialization to nonreduced fibers.</p>
    <p xml:id="grf-basechange-map">For a field extension <m>K/k</m>, define
      <md number="no">b_{K/k}:R_k\longrightarrow R_K,\qquad [X]\longmapsto[(X\times_kK)_{\mathrm{red}}].</md>
      Closed immersions and their open complements commute with base change, as do products. Hence the formula respects the relations and multiplication. It sends <m>\LL_k</m> to <m>\LL_K</m>, so also defines <m>\mathcal M_k\to\mathcal M_K</m>.</p>
    <p xml:id="anchor-grf-real">This map need not be injective over a nonclosed field. The element <m>[\Spec\C]-2\in R_{\R}</m> becomes zero over <m>\C</m>, because <m>\C\otimes_{\R}\C\simeq\C\times\C</m>. Over <m>\R</m>, compactly supported Euler characteristic of real points sends it to <m>-2</m>, proving that it is nonzero. This additive multiplicative realization is an external input; a stronger polynomial-valued realization is constructed in <xref ref="grf-bib-mccrory-parusinski"/>.</p>
    <p xml:id="margin-grf-real">The arithmetic distinction is concrete: <m>\Spec\C</m> has one closed point of degree two, no real points, and two points after complex base change. When the smaller field is algebraically closed, a different phenomenon controls the map: every finite certificate of a scissors identity can be specialized back to that field.</p>
    <p xml:id="grf-spreading">For a finite-type <m>k</m>-algebra <m>A</m>, use the relative scissors ring <m>K_0(\Var_A)</m> of separated finite-type <m>A</m>-schemes, again ignoring nilpotents. A map <m>A\to B</m> gives base change. This relative ring allows us to spread the equations of a variety and the finitely many relations witnessing a particular equality.</p>
    <lemma xml:id="grf-finite-data">
      <statement><p>Let <m>K/k</m> be a field extension. Finitely many varieties over <m>K</m>, finitely many morphisms between them, and finitely many identities in <m>R_K</m> descend to a finitely generated <m>k</m>-subalgebra <m>A\subset K</m>. Prescribed varieties and morphisms already defined over <m>k</m> may be kept as their constant models over <m>A</m>. An identity that holds over <m>K</m> holds over some such <m>A</m> after enlarging <m>A</m> and inverting finitely many nonzero elements.</p></statement>
      <proof><p>A finite-type separated scheme is described by finitely many affine charts, finitely presented coordinate algebras, and finitely many gluing data. Their equations and the finitely many chosen morphisms use finitely many coefficients in <m>K</m>. Place these coefficients in a finitely generated subalgebra. The equations saying that morphisms compose to the identity, or that gluing maps agree, also involve finitely many coefficients; adjoining them and localizing realizes the prescribed data over a larger subalgebra.</p>
        <p>An equality in a group given by generators and relations is witnessed by a finite integral combination of defining relations. For <m>R_K</m> this involves finitely many isomorphisms and scissors decompositions. Spread their schemes, closed immersions, isomorphisms, and inverse isomorphisms simultaneously. Complements and products then supply the required relative relations. Localizing the algebra ensures any closed-immersion and gluing conditions needed by the finite presentation. The same finite relation proves the identity over that algebra.</p></proof>
    </lemma>
    <p>Nicaise's colimit theorem is a systematic formulation of this finite-data argument <xref ref="grf-bib-nicaise" detail="Proposition 2.9"/>. His formulation uses finitely generated subrings of a field; the relative <m>k</m>-algebra version above has the same proof. No resolution of singularities is required. Algebraic closedness enters in the final specialization step.</p>
    <theorem xml:id="grf-injectivity">
      <title>Injectivity over an algebraically closed field</title>
      <statement><p>Let <m>k</m> be algebraically closed, in any characteristic, and let <m>K/k</m> be any field extension. Both maps
          <md number="no">R_k\longrightarrow R_K,\qquad \mathcal M_k\longrightarrow\mathcal M_K</md>
          are injective.</p></statement>
      <proof><p>Let <m>\alpha\in R_k</m> have zero image in <m>R_K</m>. Express <m>\alpha</m> as a finite integral combination of classes of <m>k</m>-varieties. By <xref ref="grf-finite-data"/>, the equality to zero descends to a finitely generated <m>k</m>-subalgebra <m>A\subset K</m>, keeping those varieties constant. The algebra is nonzero, and the Nullstellensatz supplies a maximal ideal <m>\mathfrak m</m> with <m>A/\mathfrak m=k</m>. Taking the fiber of each scheme and each scissors relation over this <m>k</m>-point gives the original equality <m>\alpha=0</m> in <m>R_k</m>.</p>
        <p>Now let <m>\alpha/\LL_k^n\in\mathcal M_k</m> map to zero. The criterion for vanishing in a localization gives an integer <m>r\geq0</m> with <m>\LL_K^r b_{K/k}(\alpha)=0</m> in <m>R_K</m>. By the first assertion, <m>\LL_k^r\alpha=0</m> in <m>R_k</m>, which means <m>\alpha/\LL_k^n=0</m> in <m>\mathcal M_k</m>.</p></proof>
    </theorem>
    <p>The proof can specialize several identities at once, by choosing one algebra containing all the data and one <m>k</m>-point. It provides a specialization for those objects and equalities, without constructing a homomorphism <m>R_K\to R_k</m> on all classes simultaneously.</p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="grf-properties-under-extension">
    <title>Equalities, inverses, and annihilating witnesses</title>
    <p xml:id="grf-finite-consequences">Suppose now that <m>k</m> is algebraically closed. Injectivity tests both vanishing and nonvanishing in <m>R_k</m>, and reflects each equation <m>\alpha^n=0</m>. It consequently reflects nilpotence as well. The localized injection gives
      <md number="no">X\sim_{\LL}Y\text{ over }k\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad X_K\sim_{\LL}Y_K\text{ over }K</md>
      for varieties already defined over <m>k</m>. The same equality and nilpotence conclusions hold in <m>\mathcal M_k</m>.</p>
    <p xml:id="grf-units-reflect">Invertibility is also reflected, although the inverse need not initially descend from <m>k</m>. If <m>b_{K/k}(\alpha)\beta=1</m>, express <m>\beta</m> as a finite integral combination of variety classes over <m>K</m>. Spread that expression together with the identity by <xref ref="grf-finite-data"/>, keeping <m>\alpha</m> constant, and specialize at a <m>k</m>-point. The specialized expression is an inverse of <m>\alpha</m>. Conversely any inverse over <m>k</m> remains an inverse after base change.</p>
    <p xml:id="grf-appendix">For zero divisors, the witness includes a nonvanishing condition. If <m>\alpha\beta=0</m> with <m>\alpha,\beta\ne0</m> in <m>R_k</m>, injectivity preserves both factors, so a known zero divisor remains a zero divisor over <m>K</m>. To reverse this argument, however, an annihilator over <m>K</m> need not belong to the image of <m>R_k</m>. Spreading the identity and specializing the witness preserves the equation <m>\alpha\beta=0</m>, but does not ensure that the specialized <m>\beta</m> is nonzero.</p>
    <p xml:id="grf-transfer-scope">A realization can sometimes supply this missing nonvanishing argument: if it assigns the witness a fixed nonzero numerical value and a specialization theorem preserves that value, the specialized witness remains nonzero. Such a theorem must apply to the actual family and invariant. The finite-data proof alone therefore does not establish preservation of regularity from <m>R_k</m> to <m>R_K</m>; it also does not exhibit a class that acquires an annihilator. The corresponding quantified question is recorded in <xref ref="grv-ch-questions"/>.</p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="grf-pointless-curves">
    <title>Curves without rational points</title>
    <p><idx>Severi–Brauer scheme</idx>The curve calculation in <xref ref="grv-zeta-smooth-projective-curve"/> uses a rational point both to normalize a Poincaré line bundle and to identify the schemes <m>\Pic^n(C)</m> with the Jacobian. Without such a point, an Abel–Jacobi map may become a projective bundle only after extending the field. Litt proves rationality with a denominator determined by a divisor defined over <m>k</m>.<fn>Mustaţă's <xref ref="grv-bib-Mustata" detail="Theorem 7.33 and footnote 1"/> assumes a perfect field and a rational point, and uses the modified Grothendieck ring. The footnote corrects the projective-bundle argument in an earlier version. Litt's later theorem supplies rationality without a rational point.</fn></p>
    <theorem xml:id="grf-litt-rationality">
      <title>Litt</title>
      <statement><p>Let <m>k</m> be any field and <m>C/k</m> a smooth projective geometrically connected curve. If <m>D</m> is a nonzero effective divisor on <m>C</m> of degree <m>d&gt;0</m>, then
        <md number="no">(1-t^d)(1-\LL_k^dt^d)\zeta_C(t)\in R_k[t].</md>
        In particular, <m>\zeta_C(t)</m> is rational at the origin in the ordinary Grothendieck ring, whether or not <m>C(k)</m> is empty.</p></statement>
    </theorem>
    <p>This is <xref ref="grf-bib-litt" detail="arXiv version, Theorem 10 and its proof"/>. Write <m>g</m> for the genus of <m>C</m>. For <m>n\geq\max\{0,2g-1\}</m>, the Abel map <m>\Sym^nC\to\Pic^n(C)</m> is a Severi–Brauer scheme: étale locally it is a projective-space bundle. Its Brauer class records the obstruction to descending the local universal line bundles. Litt proves that Severi–Brauer schemes over the same base, with the same Brauer class and relative dimension, have equal classes in <m>R_k</m> <xref ref="grf-bib-litt" detail="arXiv version, Proposition 27"/>.</p>
    <p>In Litt's proof, tensoring with <m>\mathcal O_C(D)</m> translates <m>\Pic^n(C)</m> to <m>\Pic^{n+d}(C)</m>. Multiplication by the section defining <m>D</m> gives an inclusion of the corresponding twisted bundles, with a rank-<m>d</m> quotient <m>Q_n</m>. Put <m>a_n=[\Sym^nC]</m> and <m>b_n=[\PP(Q_n)]</m>. Litt's projectivization identities and the equality of Severi–Brauer classes give
        <md number="no">a_{n+d}=\LL_k^da_n+b_n,\qquad b_{n+d}=b_n</md>
        for sufficiently large <m>n</m> <xref ref="grf-bib-litt" detail="arXiv version, Theorem 24, Proposition 27, and the proof of Theorem 10"/>. Subtracting two consecutive identities of step <m>d</m> gives
        <md number="no">a_{n+2d}-(1+\LL_k^d)a_{n+d}+\LL_k^da_n=0.</md>
        This is the recurrence encoded by the two denominator factors. A closed point always provides such a divisor. If <m>C(k)\ne\varnothing</m>, one can take <m>d=1</m> and recover the denominator used in <xref ref="grv-zeta-smooth-projective-curve"/>.</p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="grf-complex-models">
    <title>Complex models and uniform relations</title>
    <p>Any finite collection of varieties, morphisms, and equations over a characteristic-zero field descends to a subfield <m>F</m> finitely generated over <m>\Q</m>. Choose algebraically independent complex values for a transcendence basis and extend the embedding across the remaining finite algebraic extension; this embeds <m>F</m> into <m>\C</m>. The construction can now be studied through a complex model.</p>
    <p>A transfer back to the original field still uses the particular hypotheses and conclusion. For example, the varieties and extra parameters introduced by a complex construction must first have a common field of definition. That field need not be contained in the original <m>k</m>. Spreading the construction and choosing a fiber requires a preservation argument for every desired property, including nonvanishing if an annihilator is involved. This explains why one can transport numerical Hodge polynomials more directly than arguments about integral Hodge structures.</p>
    <proposition xml:id="grf-hodge-polynomial">
      <statement><p>For every characteristic-zero field <m>k</m> there is a ring homomorphism
          <md number="no">E_k:R_k\longrightarrow\Z[u,v]</md>
          whose value on a smooth projective variety is
          <md number="no">E_k(X;u,v)=\sum_{p,q}(-1)^{p+q}\dim_k H^q(X,\Omega^p_{X/k})u^pv^q.</md>
          It sends <m>\LL_k</m> to <m>uv</m>. On a complex variety it is the usual Hodge–Deligne polynomial.</p></statement>
      <proof><p>For smooth projective varieties the displayed dimensions commute with extension of the ground field. After passage to a complex model, the projective bundle, blowup, and Künneth formulas show that the expression respects the blowup relations and products. These equalities of integers therefore hold over the original field. Bittner's presentation extends the assignment uniquely to <m>R_k</m>. The equality <m>[\mathbb A^1]=[\mathbb P^1]-1</m> computes its value on <m>\LL_k</m>. Over <m>\C</m> the usual compact support definition gives the same values on smooth projective generators, hence the same homomorphism. See <xref ref="grv-bib-Bittner"/> and <xref ref="grf-bib-deligne"/> for the two inputs.</p></proof>
    </proposition>
    <p xml:id="anchor-grf-lefschetz">The polynomial is thus defined without a specified embedding <m>k\hookrightarrow\C</m>, even when the cardinality of <m>k</m> prevents such an embedding. An actual integral or rational Hodge structure requires a complex realization of a suitable model and contains information beyond the numbers <m>\dim H^q(X,\Omega^p)</m>. The polynomial argument does not transfer every assertion about an integral Hodge lattice.</p>
    <aside xml:id="margin-grf-lefschetz"><title>Lefschetz principle</title><p>The first-order theory of algebraically closed fields of a fixed characteristic is complete. Quantification over all classes and all annihilators of an infinitely presented Grothendieck ring is not automatically a first-order formula in the language of fields.</p></aside>
    <p xml:id="grf-cardinality">The existence of common characteristic-zero constructions also does not make the rings themselves isomorphic. Elliptic curves already give a lower bound on their cardinalities that matches the elementary upper bound from finite presentations.</p>
    <proposition xml:id="grf-ring-cardinality">
      <statement><p>If <m>k</m> is algebraically closed of characteristic zero, then <m>|R_k|=|k|</m>. In particular <m>R_{\overline{\Q}}</m> and <m>R_{\C}</m> are not isomorphic as abstract rings.</p></statement>
      <proof><p>An infinite field <m>k</m> has <m>|k|</m> finite systems of polynomial equations, so there are at most <m>|k|</m> finite-type affine schemes up to isomorphism. Finitely many charts and gluing maps still give at most <m>|k|</m> finite-type separated schemes. The free abelian group on their classes has cardinality at most <m>|k|</m>, and so does its quotient <m>R_k</m>.</p>
        <p>For the reverse inequality, every <m>j\in k</m> occurs as the <m>j</m>-invariant of an elliptic curve over <m>k</m>. Curves with different <m>j</m>-invariants are nonisomorphic and not stably birational: the Albanese variety is a stable birational invariant. The Larsen–Lunts identification <m>R_k/(\LL_k)\simeq\Z[\mathrm{SB}_k]</m> therefore makes their classes distinct. This gives at least <m>|k|</m> elements in <m>R_k</m>. The theorem used in this step is characteristic zero; see <xref ref="grv-bib-LL03"/>. Finally <m>\overline{\Q}</m> is countable and <m>\C</m> is uncountable.</p></proof>
    </proposition>
    <p>Different transcendence degrees can give fields of the same cardinality, so this calculation does not distinguish every pair of algebraically closed characteristic-zero fields. It does distinguish <m>R_{\overline{\Q}}</m> from <m>R_{\C}</m>, even though many geometric theorems hold in both settings.</p>
    <p>Countable models also give a uniformity result for families. A complex point can be generic over a countable field of definition. Applying the pointwise hypothesis at that point and spreading the resulting finite certificate produces one exponent on a dense open subset; induction then controls the remaining fibers.</p>
    <proposition xml:id="grf-uniform-family-exponent">
      <title>Uniform exponents in complex families</title>
      <statement><p>Let <m>\mathcal X,\mathcal Y\to S</m> be smooth projective families over a finite-type complex scheme. Suppose that <m>\mathcal X_s\sim_{\LL}\mathcal Y_s</m> for every <m>s\in S(\C)</m>. Then there is an integer <m>N\geq0</m> such that
        <md number="no">\LL^N\bigl([\mathcal X_s]-[\mathcal Y_s]\bigr)=0\quad\text{in }R_{\C}\qquad\text{for every }s\in S(\C).</md></p></statement>
      <proof>
        <p>Replacing <m>S</m> by its reduction does not affect its complex fibers. We first prove that, for integral <m>S</m>, one exponent works over a nonempty open subset. Descend <m>S</m>, both families, and their projective embeddings to a countable algebraically closed subfield <m>k_0\subset\C</m>. Such a field is obtained by adjoining the finitely many coefficients of the data to <m>\Q</m> and taking their algebraic closure in <m>\C</m>. Denote the models by <m>S_0,\mathcal X_0,\mathcal Y_0</m>. The scheme <m>S_0</m> is integral because its faithfully flat extension <m>S</m> is integral. Put <m>F=k_0(S_0)</m>.</p>
        <p>Since <m>k_0</m> is countable and <m>F/k_0</m> is finitely generated, choose an embedding <m>F\hookrightarrow\C</m> over <m>k_0</m>. It gives a point <m>s\in S(\C)</m> projecting to the generic point of <m>S_0</m>. This is a closed complex point of <m>S</m>, so the hypothesis applies to it. Identify <m>F</m> with its image and let <m>\Omega\subset\C</m> be its algebraic closure there. The generic fibers over <m>\Omega</m> become <m>\mathcal X_s,\mathcal Y_s</m> after extension to <m>\C</m>. For some <m>n\geq0</m> their difference is killed by <m>\LL^n</m> over <m>\C</m>. Injectivity of <m>R_{\Omega}\to R_{\C}</m>, from <xref ref="grf-injectivity"/>, reflects that equality to <m>R_{\Omega}</m>.</p>
        <p>The equality has a finite certificate in scissors relations. By <xref ref="grf-finite-data"/> it descends to a finitely generated <m>F</m>-subalgebra of <m>\Omega</m>. Since <m>\Omega/F</m> is algebraic, this subalgebra is a finite field extension <m>F'/F</m>. Choose an affine open <m>\Spec A\subset S_0</m>. In characteristic zero write <m>F'=F(\theta)</m>. After inverting a nonzero element of <m>A</m>, the coefficients of a monic polynomial for <m>\theta</m> lie in <m>A</m>; thus <m>B=A[\theta]</m> is a finite integral <m>A</m>-algebra with fraction field <m>F'</m>. The morphism <m>f:T=\Spec B\to\Spec A</m> is finite and surjective.</p>
        <p>Spread the certificate over a dense open <m>V\subset T</m>, using the pullbacks of <m>\mathcal X_0,\mathcal Y_0</m> as the prescribed models. The relative finite-data argument gives
          <md number="no">\LL_V^n\bigl([\mathcal X_0\times_{S_0}V]-[\mathcal Y_0\times_{S_0}V]\bigr)=0\quad\text{in }K_0(\Var_V).</md>
          Because <m>f</m> is finite, <m>f(T\setminus V)</m> is closed. It misses the generic point of <m>\Spec A</m>, whose inverse image is the single generic point of <m>T</m>. Its complement <m>U_0\subset\Spec A</m> is therefore nonempty and satisfies <m>T\times_{S_0}U_0\subset V</m>.</p>
        <p>Set <m>U=U_0\times_{k_0}\C\subset S</m>. For every <m>u\in U(\C)</m>, the fiber of the finite surjective cover over <m>u</m> is a nonempty finite complex scheme and hence has a complex point <m>t</m>. Taking the fiber of the relative identity at <m>t</m> gives
          <md number="no">\LL^n\bigl([\mathcal X_u]-[\mathcal Y_u]\bigr)=0.</md>
          Thus the same exponent works on all of <m>U(\C)</m>. This uses lifts of complex points; it does not assert a relative identity on <m>U</m> itself.</p>
        <p>For general reduced <m>S</m>, apply this argument to each of its finitely many irreducible components and choose the open subsets away from their intersections. Their union is open in <m>S</m> and dense in every component. The maximum of the finitely many exponents works there. The closed complement has smaller dimension, and the pointwise hypothesis holds for the restricted families. Induction on dimension gives an exponent for that complement. Taking the maximum again proves the assertion on <m>S</m>; the empty base is immediate.</p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
    <p>The argument gives finitely many locally closed pieces with annihilating exponents, and hence a uniform upper bound. It gives no explicit numerical bound from the dimension or equations of the families. Such quantitative bounds are a further question, distinct from the existence just proved.</p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="grf-characteristic">
    <title>What changes in positive characteristic?</title>
    <p xml:id="grf-positive-basic">The scissors ring, Zariski locally trivial fibration formulas, and the base-change injection above are available in every characteristic. Over a perfect field, every variety also has a finite smooth stratification: the smooth locus contains a dense open subset of each irreducible component, and induction applies to the lower-dimensional complement.</p>
    <p>Smooth strata alone do not provide smooth projective generators with a complete set of blowup relations. Bittner's characteristic-zero theorem uses resolution, compactification, and weak factorization; Larsen–Lunts uses that presentation to compute the stable birational quotient. Their characteristic-zero hypotheses are therefore retained in these notes.</p>
    <p xml:id="grf-bittner-ring-warning">A Bittner presentation ring can be defined in positive characteristic using smooth complete varieties and prescribed blowup relations. Sending each generator to its variety class gives a map to <m>R_k</m>, since blowups satisfy scissors relations. Without a presentation theorem that map need not be an isomorphism. Joshi studies precisely such a smooth-complete presentation ring <xref ref="grf-bib-joshi"/>. A zero divisor proved nonzero there requires a new nonvanishing argument after mapping to the usual scissors ring.</p>
    <p xml:id="grf-coefficient-characteristic">The characteristic of the field is also separate from the characteristic of its Grothendieck ring. For algebraically closed <m>k</m>, choose a prime <m>\ell\ne\charac k</m>. Compactly supported étale Euler characteristic gives
      <md number="no">\chi_{c,\ell}(X)=\sum_i(-1)^i\dim_{\Q_\ell}H_c^i(X,\Q_\ell),\qquad \chi_{c,\ell}:R_k\longrightarrow\Z.</md>
      Localization for a closed subvariety and Künneth give additivity and multiplicativity; a point maps to <m>1</m>. Thus <m>n\cdot1=0</m> in <m>R_k</m> implies <m>n=0</m>, and <m>R_k</m> has characteristic zero. The cohomological input is the compact-support formalism of <xref ref="grf-bib-sga45"/>. Reducing coefficients modulo <m>p</m> or tensoring with <m>\Q</m> is a further operation, even when <m>\charac k=p</m>.</p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="grf-universal-homeomorphisms">
    <title>Universal homeomorphisms and the modified ring</title>
    <p>Purely inseparable morphisms lead to a further comparison in positive characteristic. Nilpotents have already disappeared by scissors relations, but reduced schemes related by a purely inseparable finite morphism are not formally identified by that observation.</p>
    <definition xml:id="grf-modified-ring">
      <statement><p>A morphism <m>f:X\to Y</m> is a <em>universal homeomorphism</em> if every base change is a homeomorphism on underlying topological spaces. For finite-type schemes over a field, these are exactly the finite, surjective, radicial morphisms. Let <m>I_{\mathrm{uh}}</m> be the ideal of <m>R_k</m> generated by <m>[X]-[Y]</m> for such morphisms. The modified Grothendieck ring is
          <md number="no">R_k^{\mathrm{mod}}=R_k/I_{\mathrm{uh}}.</md></p></statement>
    </definition>
    <p>For example, over an algebraically closed field of characteristic <m>p</m>, the morphism <m>\mathbb A^1\to\mathbb A^1</m>, <m>t\mapsto t^p</m>, is a universal homeomorphism without being an isomorphism. Its source and target are already isomorphic varieties, so it gives no distinction between the two rings. For a general variety, the relative Frobenius <m>X\to X^{(p)}</m> is the appropriate <m>k</m>-morphism; absolute Frobenius need not be defined over <m>k</m>.</p>
    <proposition xml:id="grf-charzero-uh">
      <statement><p>If <m>k</m> has characteristic zero, then <m>R_k\to R_k^{\mathrm{mod}}</m> is an isomorphism.</p></statement>
      <proof><p>After ignoring nilpotents, consider a finite universal homeomorphism <m>X\to Y</m>. On corresponding irreducible components it gives a purely inseparable extension of function fields, hence an equality of function fields in characteristic zero. A finite birational morphism is an isomorphism over a dense open subset. Remove these open subsets from the corresponding components and repeat on the lower-dimensional complements. Noetherian induction gives stratifications of <m>X</m> and <m>Y</m> by pairwise isomorphic locally closed pieces. Their classes are equal by scissors relations. Thus every generator of <m>I_{\mathrm{uh}}</m> is zero. This is the argument of <xref ref="grf-bib-nicaise-sebag" detail="Propositions 3.1 and 3.3"/>.</p></proof>
    </proposition>
    <p>In positive characteristic a purely inseparable function-field extension need not be trivial, so the proof no longer applies. Standard étale realizations are invariant under universal homeomorphisms and factor through <m>R_k^{\mathrm{mod}}</m> <xref ref="grf-bib-nicaise-sebag" detail="Section 4"/>. Agreement under these realizations cannot distinguish a nonzero generator of <m>I_{\mathrm{uh}}</m> in the ordinary ring.</p>
    <theorem xml:id="grf-conditional-uh">
      <title>Conditional preprint result, October 2026</title>
      <statement><p>Annala–Satriano–Sundbo claim the following in arXiv:2610.02690v2. Suppose <m>k</m> is perfect of positive characteristic and satisfies weak resolution in the sense that every reduced finite-type <m>k</m>-scheme <m>X</m> admits a projective birational morphism <m>X'\to X</m> with <m>X'</m> smooth over <m>k</m>. Then there are smooth projective surfaces <m>X,Y</m> over <m>k</m> that are universally homeomorphic but have <m>[X]\neq[Y]</m> in <m>R_k</m>. Under this hypothesis, <m>R_k\to R_k^{\mathrm{mod}}</m> is not injective.</p></statement>
    </theorem>
    <p>The source is <xref ref="grf-bib-annala-satriano-sundbo" detail="Theorem C, Proposition 3.20, and Section 5"/>, version 2 of 13 October 2026. Its weak-resolution hypothesis concerns all dimensions; resolution just for the displayed surfaces would not suffice for this argument about relations in the full ring. The noninjectivity conclusion follows from the definition of the modified ring. We record this recent conditional preprint result without reconstructing its motivic proof.</p>
    <p>The proposed construction retains invariants at the characteristic, including Hodge numbers, that can distinguish universally homeomorphic surfaces. It therefore uses information absent from the étale realizations that already factor through the modified ring. Both the ordinary-ring target and the resolution hypothesis are part of the stated comparison.</p>
  </section>
</chapter>
